# ── deploy/.env ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to deploy/.env and fill in. That file is gitignored; this template is not.
#
#   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env
#
# Every value here is a SECRET or a store assignment. deploy/.env is the only
# place the Shopify credentials live on the box.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is nothing to look up here — this database does not exist yet, and this
# value CREATES its password on first boot. It is unrelated to the Postgres your
# local server/.env points at. Generate one, don't invent one:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Postgres only reads this when it initializes an EMPTY data directory. Change it
# later and the container keeps the old one, and every instance fails with
# "password authentication failed". To actually change it you must destroy the
# volume: docker compose -f deploy/docker-compose.yml down -v
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ── Per-SE store assignments ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THIS IS THE ISOLATION BOUNDARY. Each variable is the complete list of stores one
# SE can reach. getShopifyConfig() looks a requested storeId up in THIS instance's
# list and throws ShopifyConfigError when it is not there, so an SE cannot drive a
# store that is not in their own variable. That is what makes this work with no
# login at all.
#
#   GIVE EACH STORE TO EXACTLY ONE SE.
#
# List one store under two SEs and you have handed two people the same store. The
# busy-lock will stop them colliding at the same instant — not five minutes apart,
# while one of them is still reading their report.
#
# ── How strong that boundary is depends on which credential you use ──────────
#
# Both styles below work. They do NOT give the same guarantee.
#
#   adminToken, one per store
#     The instance holds a credential for its own stores and nothing else. Another
#     SE's store is unreachable because no key for it exists in the process. A
#     mistyped entry fails CLOSED: validateStore rejects a store with no
#     credential, at boot, before anything serves.
#
#   clientId + clientSecret, one app shared across every store
#     A token is minted per shop at runtime from (clientId, clientSecret, shop) —
#     see fetchClientCredentialsToken() in services/shopifyClient.ts. The only
#     per-store input is the domain. So if one custom app is installed across all
#     the test stores, every container holds a credential that could reach ANY of
#     them, and this list is the only thing scoping it. Adding one line to the
#     wrong SE's variable silently GRANTS access instead of failing.
#
# For a small internal team whose failure mode is mistakes rather than attackers,
# the shared app is a reasonable trade, and it makes scopes one thing to manage
# instead of one per store. Just don't mistake it for the stronger boundary:
# under it, THIS FILE IS A MASTER KEY. chmod 600 it. Don't paste it into chat.
#
# Format: a JSON array. Each store needs `shop` plus either `adminToken` or
# `clientId` + `clientSecret`. `label` is what the SE sees in the picker; `id` is
# derived from the shop domain when omitted. Single-quoted multi-line values work
# (verified against docker compose), so pretty-printed JSON is fine.
# Full schema: server/src/config/shopify.ts

# ── Style A — one shared custom app, client credentials ─────────────────────
# This is what server/.env already uses.
SHOPIFY_STORES_SE1='[{"label":"Anna QA 1","shop":"anna-qa-1.myshopify.com","clientId":"REPLACE","clientSecret":"REPLACE"},{"label":"Anna QA 2","shop":"anna-qa-2.myshopify.com","clientId":"REPLACE","clientSecret":"REPLACE"}]'

SHOPIFY_STORES_SE2='[{"label":"SE2 QA 1","shop":"se2-qa-1.myshopify.com","clientId":"REPLACE","clientSecret":"REPLACE"}]'

SHOPIFY_STORES_SE3='[{"label":"SE3 QA 1","shop":"se3-qa-1.myshopify.com","clientId":"REPLACE","clientSecret":"REPLACE"}]'

SHOPIFY_STORES_SE4='[{"label":"SE4 QA 1","shop":"se4-qa-1.myshopify.com","clientId":"REPLACE","clientSecret":"REPLACE"}]'

SHOPIFY_STORES_SE5='[{"label":"SE5 QA 1","shop":"se5-qa-1.myshopify.com","clientId":"REPLACE","clientSecret":"REPLACE"}]'

SHOPIFY_STORES_SE6='[{"label":"SE6 QA 1","shop":"se6-qa-1.myshopify.com","clientId":"REPLACE","clientSecret":"REPLACE"}]'

SHOPIFY_STORES_SE7='[{"label":"SE7 QA 1","shop":"se7-qa-1.myshopify.com","clientId":"REPLACE","clientSecret":"REPLACE"}]'

# ── Style B — per-store admin token (stronger boundary, more setup) ─────────
# Create a custom app in each store's own admin (Apps → Develop apps) and paste
# that store's Admin API access token. No client secret ever reaches the box, so
# a container literally cannot authenticate against a store it was not given.
#
# SHOPIFY_STORES_SE1='[{"label":"Anna QA 1","shop":"anna-qa-1.myshopify.com","adminToken":"shpat_REPLACE"}]'

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Admin API version for every store that does not override it.
SHOPIFY_API_VERSION=2026-01

# Prisma pool size PER INSTANCE. Seven instances share one Postgres, so the total
# is seven times this. Default max_connections on postgres:16 is 100.
DATABASE_CONNECTION_LIMIT=5

# Host port for Postgres, for psql and backups only — the app containers reach it
# over the compose network and ignore this. Defaults to 5433 so it cannot collide
# with a local development Postgres on 5432. On a dedicated box, 5432 is fine.
POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=5433

# The seven instances publish on 3101-3107, NOT 3001-3007: 3001 is the development
# API port (see CLAUDE.md), and colliding with it would break `npm run dev` on the
# same machine. To change them, edit the port mappings in docker-compose.yml.

# Which interface the published ports bind to. Defaults to 127.0.0.1 — nothing is
# reachable from the network until you change it, which is the right default for a
# stack with NO AUTHENTICATION.
#
# The app reads this too (server/src/index.ts), so it also applies to a native
# non-Docker deployment, where it is the only lock besides the host firewall.
# Setting 0.0.0.0 exposes every destructive route to anyone who can reach the box.
# Read the warning block at the top of docker-compose.yml before you do.
BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THE SHARED DATABASE MEANS
#
# All five instances share one database, so everyone sees everyone's history in
# one place — including you. That is the point of the choice. It also means:
#
#   1. ANY SE CAN DELETE ANY RUN. DELETE /api/customer-validation/:id and
#      /api/product-upload/:id take an id and check no ownership, and the history
#      UI now lists everyone's runs. One click, no undo.
#
#   2. Foreign runs are visible but not actionable. An SE can open a colleague's
#      run and press import or cleanup; their instance has no config for that
#      store, so it fails with a config error rather than doing anything. Safe,
#      but the message will not explain why.
#
#   3. Your logs will be noisy. sweepRunningCleanups runs every 60s in every
#      container and reconciles cleanup runs for stores it cannot reach. It
#      catches and logs; it changes no state. Expect the error lines.
#
# Want none of that? Give each instance its own DATABASE_URL (append a distinct
# database name) and run migrate per database. You lose the single history view.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BEFORE YOU EXPOSE THIS
#
# There is no authentication. Ports bind to 127.0.0.1 only, so out of the box
# nothing is reachable from off the host. Keep it that way:
#
#   Tailscale (simplest):  tailscale up   then SEs reach http://<host>:3101..3105
#   VPN:                   put the box on it, do not open the ports
#   Cloudflare Tunnel:     point it at 127.0.0.1:310X
#
# Do NOT change the bindings to 0.0.0.0 and put this on public DNS. Six routes
# destroy things and four delete records by tag across an entire Shopify store.
# See the warning at the top of docs/DEPLOY.md.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
